WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.936
Testing should begin away from production.

2
00:00:02.936 --> 00:00:17.220
A lab or staging environment lets teams validate protocol support, library behavior, certificate handling, client compatibility, performance, logging, and operational dashboards before users are affected.

3
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:33.011
For TLS, it is important to test real client types: browsers, mobile apps, partner SDKs, command-line tools, service clients, monitoring agents, proxies, and any enterprise interception or inspection components that sit in the path.

4
00:00:33.011 --> 00:00:37.375
Performance testing should measure the parts that could change.

5
00:00:37.375 --> 00:00:49.596
Teams should look at handshake size, handshake latency, CPU use, memory, connection rates, certificate chain size, retry behavior, failure rates, and effects on high-volume APIs.

6
00:00:49.596 --> 00:00:56.023
A small increase in handshake overhead may be acceptable for one service and costly for another.

7
00:00:56.023 --> 00:01:03.641
Measure under realistic load, with realistic clients, through the same gateways and proxies used in production.

8
00:01:03.641 --> 00:01:05.625
Deployment should be staged.

9
00:01:05.625 --> 00:01:17.210
Canary releases, limited endpoint groups, internal-only pilots, regional rollouts, or opt-in client populations can expose compatibility issues before a broad change.

10
00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:21.257
Observability matters as much as cryptographic selection.

11
00:01:21.257 --> 00:01:39.032
Teams should alert on TLS negotiation failures, certificate validation errors, handshake latency, client disconnects, elevated retries, unexpected fallback behavior, and service-level impacts that might otherwise look like generic availability problems.

12
00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:43.159
Rollback planning is part of responsible change management.

13
00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:55.776
A team should know what setting to reverse, who approves the rollback, what logs must be preserved, and how to avoid oscillating between configurations without understanding the failure.

14
00:01:55.776 --> 00:02:02.045
After deployment, monitoring should continue because client populations change over time.

15
00:02:02.045 --> 00:02:11.012
PQC readiness for TLS is operational discipline: test carefully, deploy gradually, observe clearly, and document what was learned.
